  "executive_briefing": "As of July 2026, international tax policy and cross-border trade operations face major structural shifts driven by trade protectionism and multilateral tax standardization. Unilateral tariffs in the United States have escalated sharply following court rulings limiting Emergency Economic Powers Act (IEEPA) duties and shifting emphasis toward Section 232 tariffs and broad multi-nation import duties affecting 60 trade partners. Concurrently, the OECD Pillar Two 15% global minimum tax regime continues its statutory rollout across more than 140 jurisdictions, altering multinational corporate tax strategies. In response, regional hubs like Singapore and ASEAN partners are recalibrating corporate tax incentives using non-taxable CIT rebates and cash grants to maintain FDI attractiveness while advancing mandatory Peppol-based e-invoicing frameworks (e.g., InvoiceNow) for real-time GST enforcement.",
